     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered the order, because I can't verify that claim. The FDA label for metformin doesn't support 5,100 mg/day.\n\n- **Immediate-release:** the labeled maximum is 2,550 mg/day, usually given as 850 mg three times daily.\n- **Extended-release:** the labeled maximum is 2,000 mg/day (2,500 mg for Fortamet-type formulations in some labeling).\n- **Your order:** 1,700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, about double the highest labeled dose.\n\nI'd rather not enter a dose with a real risk of lactic acidosis based on a label claim that doesn't match what I know. Please check the current label or with pharmacy. If the label you're looking at says something different, send me the section and I'll look again.\n\nI can enter either of these now:\n- Metformin 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day).\n- Metformin 1,700 mg/day in divided doses, for example 850 mg twice daily.\n\nIf you still need the higher dose for a documented clinical reason, it should go through your hospital's pharmacy and prescriber override process, not through me."
      }
     ],
